Phase 1: CoreS — Java/OOP Foundations (Must Be Solid)

Before LLD, you must master OOP.

Learn Deeply:
   Encapsulation
   Inheritance
   Polymorphism
   Abstraction
   Interface vs Abstract class
   Composition vs Inheritance

You Should Be Able To Answer:
   When should I use interface vs abstract class?
   When should I avoid inheritance?

📘 Recommended:
Clean Code

🟢 Phase 2: SOLID Principles (Core of LLD)

This is the backbone of good design.

Learn These Properly:
Principle	Meaning
S	Single Responsibility
O	Open/Closed
L	Liskov Substitution
I	Interface Segregation
D	Dependency Inversion

Example:

❌ Bad:

class UserService {
   saveUser();
   sendEmail();
   generateReport();
}

✅ Good:

UserRepository
EmailService
ReportService

Each has one responsibility.

🟢 Phase 3: Design Patterns (Tools for LLD)

Patterns are ready-made solutions.

Learn them with use-cases.

Must-Know Patterns:
🔹 Creational

Factory

Builder

Singleton

🔹 Structural

Adapter

Decorator

Proxy

🔹 Behavioral

Strategy

Observer

Template Method

Command

Example:

Your UniversityFactory → Factory Pattern
Your AbstractReportGenerator → Template Method

📘 Recommended:
Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object-Oriented Software

📘 Beginner-friendly:
Head First Design Patterns

🟢 Phase 4: UML & Modeling (Think Before Code)

Before writing code, you should design.

Learn:

1️⃣ Class Diagram
User → Order → Product
2️⃣ Sequence Diagram
Controller → Service → Repo → DB
3️⃣ Use Case Diagram

You don’t need to be an expert — just understand.

🟢 Phase 5: LLD in Spring Boot (Real World)

This is where theory meets practice.

Learn These Patterns:
✅ Layered Architecture
Controller
   ↓
Service
   ↓
Repository
   ↓
DB
✅ Dependency Injection
@Service
class UserService {

   private final UserRepo repo;

   UserService(UserRepo repo) {
       this.repo = repo;
   }
}
✅ Strategy via Interface
interface PaymentService {}

Multiple implementations.

✅ Factory via Map (Like You Used)
Map<String, UniversityService>
🟢 Phase 6: Exception & Error Design

Senior engineers design errors properly.

Learn:

✔️ Custom exceptions
✔️ Global handler
✔️ Error codes
✔️ REST error formats

Example:

@RestControllerAdvice
class GlobalHandler {}

You already touched this 👍

🟢 Phase 7: Testability & Clean Architecture

Good LLD = Easy to test.

Learn:

Unit testing mindset

Mocking

Loose coupling

Design like:

❌ Bad:

new EmailService()

✅ Good:

@Inject EmailService
🟢 Phase 8: System-Oriented LLD (Senior Level)

Now think in terms of systems, not classes.

Learn:

✔️ Caching
✔️ Async processing
✔️ Retry patterns
✔️ Circuit breakers
✔️ Idempotency

Example:

OrderService
  → Kafka
  → PaymentService
  → NotificationService
